  This page gives the exact statement proved by OpenAI's Lean formalization
  (released as <hlink|<verbatim|OpenAI-YM-20261028>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/tree/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>,
  commit <verbatim|3456e5e>).

  <\enumerate>
    <item><hlink|<strong|The formal statement:>|#1-the-formal-statement> the
    Lean theorem and the definitions at its core, verbatim.

    <item><hlink|<strong|What it says, in words:>|#2-what-it-says-in-words> a
    commentary.

    <item><hlink|<strong|The precise statement:>|#3-the-precise-statement>
    the theorem restated in ordinary mathematics, definition by definition,
    with every quantifier, domain and order of limits as in the Lean.
  </enumerate>

  The <hlink|clause-to-explanation guide|#clause-to-explanation-guide> links
  the public conclusions to their outlines and the summary. The <hlink|source
  correspondence|#correspondence-with-the-lean-source> relates each item to
  its Lean declaration; <hlink|the nonclaims|#4-what-is-not-claimed>
  distinguish the result from stronger questions.

  <section|1. The formal statement><label|1-the-formal-statement>

  The headline theorem (<hlink|<verbatim|Solution.lean>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Solution.lean>):

  <\lean-code>
    /-- The core comparator asks only for existence, reconstruction, the OPE
    and the trace test. -/

    theorem YangMills.jointExistence : YangMills.JointExistence := by
  </lean-code>

  The proposition it proves (<hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L184-L188>):

  <\lean-code>
    /-- One Wilson family per gauge group, followed by a refinement for every
    requested

    volume sequence. All four conclusions concern a single hierarchy on that
    refinement. -/

    def JointExistence : Prop := \<forall\> g : GaugeGroup, g.IsSimple
    \<rightarrow\> \<exists\> C : WilsonFamily g,

    \ \ WilsonRegularity C \<wedge\> \<forall\> p : \<bbb-N\> \<rightarrow\>
    \<bbb-N\>, StrictMono p \<rightarrow\> \<exists\> q : \<bbb-N\>
    \<rightarrow\> \<bbb-N\>, StrictMono q \<wedge\>

    \ \ \ \ \<exists\> T : Hierarchy C, T.Properties (p \<circ\> q)
  </lean-code>

  The four properties required of the continuum hierarchy, along the refined
  volume sequence (<hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L177-L181>):

  <\lean-code>
    structure Properties (order : \<bbb-N\> \<rightarrow\> \<bbb-N\>) : Prop
    where

    \ \ wilson : C.Converges order (fun v =\<gtr\> T.correlation none
    (T.project \<circ\> v))

    \ \ reconstruction : T.Reconstructed

    \ \ ope : T.OperatorProductExpansion

    \ \ nonfree : T.TraceTest
  </lean-code>

  The first property, the lattice-to-continuum limit, is
  <hlink|<verbatim|WilsonFamily.Converges>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/PublicWilson.lean#L85-L90>:
  for every fixed admissible volume the smeared lattice moments converge as
  the lattice spacing tends to zero, and the limits converge along the volume
  sequence to the continuum correlation:

  <\lean-code>
    def Converges (volumeOrder : \<bbb-N\> \<rightarrow\> \<bbb-N\>)

    \ \ \ \ (unselected : {n : \<bbb-N\>} \<rightarrow\> (Fin n
    \<rightarrow\> C.Token) \<rightarrow\> Points n \<rightarrow\> \<bbb-C\>)
    : Prop :=

    \ \ \<forall\> n (v : Fin n \<rightarrow\> C.Token) (t : SeparatedTest
    n),

    \ \ \ \ (\<forall\>\<#1DA0\> k : \<bbb-N\> in atTop, C.suitable
    (volumeOrder k) t) \<wedge\> \<exists\> row : \<bbb-N\> \<rightarrow\>
    \<bbb-C\>,

    \ \ \ \ (\<forall\> p, C.suitable p t \<rightarrow\> Tendsto (fun N
    =\<gtr\> C.smear N p v t) atTop (\<b-cal-N\> (row p))) \<wedge\>

    \ \ \ \ Tendsto (fun k =\<gtr\> row (volumeOrder k)) atTop (\<b-cal-N\>
    (\<big-int\> x, t x * unselected v x))
  </lean-code>

  <\ym-details|Formal reconstruction properties (R1\UR15)>
    The second, the reconstruction of a relativistic theory
    (<hlink|<verbatim|Reconstructed>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L117-L144>):

    <\lean-code>
      structure Reconstructed : Prop where

      \ \ axioms : T.theory.Axioms

      \ \ faithful : T.theory.fields.Faithful

      \ \ injective (d : \<bbb-N\>) : Injective (T.toLabel d)

      \ \ acts : \<exists\> (a : T.Tag) (f : YangMills.TestFunction),
      T.theory.fields.field (T.toLabel a.1 a.2) f \<neq\> 0

      \ \ covers : Surjective (fun a : T.Tag =\<gtr\> T.toLabel a.1 a.2)

      \ \ represents : Surjective T.project

      \ \ normalized : IsProbabilityMeasure T.phaseMeasure

      \ \ fullSupport : T.phaseMeasure.IsOpenPosMeasure

      \ \ permutation (s : Option T.Character) {n : \<bbb-N\>} (a : Fin n
      \<rightarrow\> T.Tag)

      \ \ \ \ \ \ (x : Points n) (hx : x \<in\> WilsonFamily.separated n)
      (\<pi\> : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) :

      \ \ \ \ T.correlation s (a \<circ\> \<pi\>) (WithLp.toLp 2 (x.ofLp
      \<circ\> \<pi\>)) = T.correlation s a x

      \ \ smooth (s : Option T.Character) {n : \<bbb-N\>} (a : Fin n
      \<rightarrow\> T.Tag) :

      \ \ \ \ ContDiffOn \<bbb-R\> \<infty\> (T.correlation s a)
      (WilsonFamily.separated n)

      \ \ characterContinuity {n : \<bbb-N\>} (a : Fin n \<rightarrow\>
      T.Tag) (x : Points n)

      \ \ \ \ \ \ (h : x \<in\> WilsonFamily.separated n) : Continuous (fun i
      =\<gtr\> T.correlation (some i) a x)

      \ \ mixture {n : \<bbb-N\>} (a : Fin n \<rightarrow\> T.Tag) (x :
      Points n) (h : x \<in\> WilsonFamily.separated n) :

      \ \ \ \ (\<big-int\> i, T.correlation (some i) a x
      \<partial\>T.phaseMeasure) = T.correlation none a x

      \ \ analytic {n : \<bbb-N\>} (v : Fin n \<rightarrow\> T.Tag) (space :
      OPESpacePoints n) :

      \ \ \ \ DifferentiableOn \<bbb-C\> (fun z =\<gtr\> T.continuation v z
      space) (orderedTube n)

      \ \ root {n : \<bbb-N\>} (v : Fin n \<rightarrow\> T.Tag) (x : Points
      n) (h : StrictMono fun i =\<gtr\> time (x i)) :

      \ \ \ \ T.continuation v (imaginaryTimes x) (spatialPoints x) =
      T.correlation (some T.selected) v x

      \ \ boundary {n : \<bbb-N\>} (v : Fin n \<rightarrow\> T.Tag) (height :
      Fin n \<rightarrow\> \<bbb-R\>) (h : StrictMono height)

      \ \ \ \ \ \ (tests : Fin n \<rightarrow\> YangMills.TestFunction) :

      \ \ \ \ Tendsto (fun r =\<gtr\> \<big-int\> x : Points n,
      T.continuation v (complexShift r height x) (spatialPoints x) *

      \ \ \ \ \ \ tensorTest tests x) (\<b-cal-N\>[\<gtr\>] 0)

      \ \ \ \ \ \ (\<b-cal-N\> (T.theory.fields.vacuumExpectation (fun i
      =\<gtr\> T.toLabel (v i).1 (v i).2) tests))

      \;
    </lean-code>
  </ym-details>

  The fourth, the non-freeness test on the trace field
  (<hlink|<verbatim|TraceTest>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L168-L176>):

  <\lean-code>
    structure TraceTest : Prop where

    \ \ centered (f : YangMills.TestFunction) :

    \ \ \ \ inner \<bbb-C\> T.theory.fields.vacuum.1

    \ \ \ \ \ \ (T.theory.fields.field (T.toLabel 4 T.centeredTrace) f
    T.theory.fields.vacuum).1 = 0

    \ \ amplitudePositive : 0 \<less\> 3 * (Module.finrank \<bbb-R\>
    g.lieAlgebra : \<bbb-R\>) / Real.pi^4

    \ \ shortDistance : Tendsto (fun r : \<bbb-R\> =\<gtr\> (r^8 * (Real.log
    r\<#207B\>\<onesuperior\>)^2) \<bullet\> T.traceTwo r)

    \ \ \ \ (\<b-cal-N\>[\<gtr\>] 0) (\<b-cal-N\> ((3 * (Module.finrank
    \<bbb-R\> g.lieAlgebra : \<bbb-R\>) / Real.pi^4 : \<bbb-R\>) :
    \<bbb-C\>))

    \ \ excludes (terms : List (\<bbb-C\> \<times\> Track)) : \<neg\>
    T.traceTwo =\<#1DA0\>[\<b-cal-N\>[\<gtr\>] 0] wickProfile terms

    \;
  </lean-code>

  The third, the operator product expansion, is
  <hlink|<verbatim|OperatorProductExpansion>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L70-L94>.
  The objects these definitions use are defined in
  <hlink|<verbatim|PublicTheory.lean>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/PublicTheory.lean>
  (gauge groups, Wightman theories), <hlink|<verbatim|PublicWilson.lean>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/PublicWilson.lean>
  (lattices, the Wilson measure, Wilson families) and
  <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean>
  (regularity, the hierarchy). All three import only Mathlib.

  <section|2. What it says, in words><label|2-what-it-says-in-words>

  <strong|The main theorem.> Fix a compact group of unitary matrices G whose
  Lie algebra \<frak-g\> is simple and nonabelian, for example SU(N) with N
  \<geqslant\> 2. Then the following hold.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|One family of lattice theories.> There is one family of
    Wilson lattice gauge theories for G on four-dimensional tori:

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>lattice spacings 1/D_N \<rightarrow\> 0;

      <item>torus sides L_p \<rightarrow\> \<infty\>;

      <item>couplings \<beta\>_{N,p} with \<beta\>_{N,p}/log D_N
      \<rightarrow\> b_* \<gtr\> 0, uniformly in the volume;

      <item>local gauge-invariant insertions ("tokens"), each depending on a
      fixed finite patch of links.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|For every sequence of volumes, a subsequence.> Given any
    increasing sequence of volumes, one can pass to a subsequence along which
    the smeared correlations of the insertions converge. The limit is taken
    in two steps: first the lattice spacing tends to zero at each fixed
    volume, then the volume tends to infinity along the subsequence. The test
    functions are smooth, compactly supported, and vanish near all coincident
    points. The limits are given by correlation functions S on
    \<bbb-R\>\<#2074\> that are smooth away from coincident points.

    <item><strong|Phases.> S is an average over a compact space of
    <em|phases> \<xi\>, against a probability measure of full support, of
    phase correlations S^\<xi\> that depend continuously on \<xi\>.

    <item><strong|A relativistic theory.> One selected phase carries a
    quantum field theory in Minkowski space satisfying Wightman-type axioms:

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>a cyclic vacuum, unique up to scalars among translation-invariant
      vectors;

      <item>positive energy;

      <item>Poincaré covariance;

      <item>locality;

      <item>faithful fields.
    </itemize-minus>

    Its vacuum expectations are boundary values of analytic continuations of
    the Euclidean correlations.

    <item><strong|Short distances.> The mixed state and every phase satisfy
    operator product expansion bounds with a single system of coefficients.

    <item><strong|Not free.> A degree-4 field \<Theta\>, the centered trace
    of the stress tensor in the construction, has the short-distance law in
    the selected phase, <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>\<to\>3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>/\<pi\><rsup|4>> as <math|r\<to\>0<rsup|+>>, where
    <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> is the two-point function at times <math|-r/2>
    and <math|r/2> on the time axis. No finite Wick profile specified in
    Definition 1.15 agrees with this function on any interval
    <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>.
  </enumerate>

  <strong|What the construction gives in addition.> The detailed theorem from
  which this is derived, <hlink|<verbatim|YangMills.constructed>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Construction.lean>,
  says more:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>the constant is the asymptotic-freedom value b_* = 11 C_\<frak-g\>
    / (24\<pi\>\<twosuperior\>);

    <item><em|every> phase carries a Wightman theory;

    <item>correlations extend to distributions across coincident points;

    <item>the rescaled correlations converge at short distance to those of
    dim \<frak-g\> free Maxwell fields;

    <item>there are nested and integrated versions of the OPE, and several
    further tests of non-freeness.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|How the formulation differs from the standard one.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The limit is shown to exist only along subsequences, and only on
    separated configurations; nothing is asserted at coincident points.

    <item>Uniqueness of the limit, independence of the subsequence and a mass
    gap are not claimed.

    <item>Clustering is replaced by the decomposition into phases.

    <item>The Wightman axioms are in the formalization's own form; the
    precise statement below lists every difference.

    <item>No Osterwalder\USchrader axiom appears in the public statement.

    <item>The field labels are abstract finite-dimensional spaces; the
    identification with polynomials in the curvature is made in the
    construction, not in the statement.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Clause-to-explanation guide><label|clause-to-explanation-guide>

  Each row gives a plain explanation of a public clause, the numbered results
  in the outlines that discuss it, and the corresponding passage in the
  summary. The clause labels link to definitions below, where the exact
  hypotheses and quantifiers are stated. Unit links open \PWhat this unit
  proves\Q; the following \PWhat it uses\Q section gives its inputs. The
  <hlink|source correspondence|#correspondence-with-the-lean-source> supplies
  a pinned declaration for every clause.

  The assembly in <hlink|VIII.1|outline/units/VIII-1.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>,
  especially VIII.1.1 and VIII.1.7, connects the internal construction to
  these public properties. This is a guide to the available explanations, not
  a new audit of every dependency. III.13's coordinated family is an
  alternative construction; its properties cannot be transferred to the
  public theorem's family without identifying the two.

  <subsection|Choices, regularity and the Wilson
  limit><label|choices-regularity-and-the-wilson-limit>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Clause>>|<cell|<strong|Plain
  explanation>>|<cell|<strong|Outline results>>|<cell|<strong|Summary>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Hypothesis
  and choices|#public-theorem>>|<cell|For each matrix group with simple
