  The rotation and boost inputs are discussed in
  <hlink|VI.1|outline/units/VI-1.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves> VI.1.5\U8 and
  <hlink|VI.2|outline/units/VI-2.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves> VI.2.5\U9. The
  selected phase is chosen only for a one-dimensional ground-state space.
  Reconstruction for <strong|every> phase is an additional conclusion of the
  detailed construction, explained in <hlink|V.3|outline/units/V-3.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  V.3.1\U2; it is not a separate requirement of public P2.

  <subsection|OPE and trace test: P3 and P4><label|ope-and-trace-test-p3-and-p4>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Clause>>|<cell|<strong|Plain
  explanation>>|<cell|<strong|Outline results>>|<cell|<strong|Summary>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|P3
  / O1|#operator-product-expansion>>|<cell|Each OPE coefficient obeys a
  small-distance degree bound on separated
  clusters.>|<cell|<hlink|VII.4|outline/units/VII-4.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VII.4.1>|<cell|<hlink|OPE bounds|summary.tm#operator-product-expansion-and-contact-extensions>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|P3
  / O2|#operator-product-expansion>>|<cell|Every finite truncation has a
  remainder bound uniform in phase and allowed point positions, with one
  coefficient system for all spectators.>|<cell|<hlink|VII.4|outline/units/VII-4.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VII.4.1, 5\U6>|<cell|<hlink|OPE bounds|summary.tm#operator-product-expansion-and-contact-extensions>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|P4
  / T1|#trace-test>>|<cell|The chosen degree-4 field has zero vacuum
  expectation in the selected theory.>|<cell|<hlink|VII.2|outline/units/VII-2.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VII.2.1; <hlink|VIII.1|outline/units/VIII-1.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VIII.1.7(e)>|<cell|<hlink|Trace law and
  exclusion|summary.tm#the-trace-law-and-nonfreeness>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|P4
  / T2|#trace-test>>|<cell|The trace-law amplitude is positive for the
  allowed Lie algebras.>|<cell|<hlink|VII.2|outline/units/VII-2.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VII.2.3(a)>|<cell|<hlink|Trace law and exclusion|summary.tm#the-trace-law-and-nonfreeness>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|P4
  / T3|#trace-test>>|<cell|The selected time-axis two-point function has the
  stated positive logarithmically corrected
  asymptotic.>|<cell|<hlink|VII.2|outline/units/VII-2.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VII.2.2\U3; <hlink|VIII.1|outline/units/VIII-1.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VIII.1.7(e)>|<cell|<hlink|Trace law and
  exclusion|summary.tm#the-trace-law-and-nonfreeness>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|P4
  / T4|#trace-test>>|<cell|No specified finite Wick profile equals that
  two-point function throughout any interval next to
  zero.>|<cell|<hlink|VII.2|outline/units/VII-2.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VII.2.4; <hlink|VII.3|outline/units/VII-3.tm#1-what-this-unit-proves>
  VII.3.7 (exclusion explained in VII.2)>|<cell|<hlink|Trace law and
  exclusion|summary.tm#the-trace-law-and-nonfreeness>>>>>>

  O2 is a finite-truncation estimate; its small-radius threshold may depend
  on the spectator labels and separation parameters. It does not assert
  coefficient uniqueness, convergence of the infinite OPE or perturbative
  agreement. For T3, VII.2 supplies the scaling estimate and VIII.1 assembles
  the passage to all radii; the latter is not fully explained in the outline.
  The stress-tensor identification belongs to the construction, with symmetry
  and normalization in <hlink|VI.1|outline/units/VI-1.tm> and
  <hlink|VI.2|outline/units/VI-2.tm>, and the coupling convention in
  <hlink|VII.5|outline/units/VII-5.tm>. The public test itself constrains a
  degree-4 field and the single function in <hlink|Definition
  1.14|#centered-trace>.

  For the exact translations, see the pinned <hlink|regularity and
  reconstruction/OPE bridges|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/HierarchyBridge.lean#L16-L133>,
  <hlink|Wilson-limit bridge|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/WilsonBridge.lean#L62-L68>
  and <hlink|trace bridge|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/TraceBridge.lean#L24-L44>.

  <subsection|Additional construction conclusions
  D1\UD16><label|additional-construction-conclusions-d1d16>

  These are listed separately in <hlink|Remark 2.4|#detailed-theorem>, with
  their own review qualification. <hlink|VIII.1|outline/units/VIII-1.tm>
  describes their assembly; <hlink|V.3|outline/units/V-3.tm> explains
  every-phase reconstruction, and <hlink|VII.4|outline/units/VII-4.tm>
  explains nested and integrated OPE estimates and contact extensions. The
  summary discusses them under <hlink|assembly|summary.tm#part-viii-assembly>,
  <hlink|reconstruction|summary.tm#part-v-phases-and-relativistic-reconstruction>
  and <hlink|OPE and contacts|summary.tm#operator-product-expansion-and-contact-extensions>.
  This guide does not claim a clause-by-clause proof review of D1\UD16.

  <section|3. The precise statement><label|3-the-precise-statement>

  <subsection|0. Conventions><label|0-conventions>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><math|\<bbb-N\>=<around|{|0,1,2,\<ldots\>|}>>. "For all
    sufficiently small <math|r\<gtr\>0>" means: there is
    <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> such that the property holds for every
    <math|r\<in\><around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>. "For all sufficiently large
    <math|N>" means: there is <math|N<rsub|0>> such that it holds for every
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>.

    <item>Inner products of complex Hilbert spaces are antilinear in the
    first argument.

    <item>Integrals are Lebesgue integrals. In the formal source an integral
    of a non-integrable function is <math|0>; we point out the one place in
    the main theorem where this convention could matter (Definition 1.12,
    Remark 4).

    <item>The coordinate axes of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> are numbered
    <math|1,2,3,4>, and the <strong|fourth coordinate is time>. (The formal
    source numbers them <math|0,1,2,3>, with time the last one.)
  </itemize-dot>

  <subsection|1. The objects><label|1-the-objects>

  <subsubsection|Gauge groups><label|gauge-groups>

  <label|gauge-group> <strong|Definition 1.1 (Gauge group).> A <em|gauge
  group> is a triple <math|<around|(|m,G,\<frak-g\>|)>> consisting of an
  integer <math|m\<geqslant\>1>, a closed subgroup <math|G> of the unitary
  group <math|U<around|(|m|)>>, and a real linear subspace
  <math|\<frak-g\>\<subset\>M<rsub|m><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>> such that, for
  every <math|X\<in\>M<rsub|m><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    X\<in\>\<frak-g\>\<Longleftrightarrow\>exp <around|(|s*X|)>\<in\>G<text|
    for all >s\<in\>\<bbb-R\>.
  </equation*>

  We say that <math|\<frak-g\>> is <em|simple> if

  <\enumerate>
    <item>there are <math|X,Y\<in\>\<frak-g\>> with
    <math|<around|[|X,Y|]>\<assign\>X*Y-Y*X\<neq\>0>, and

    <item>every real linear subspace <math|I\<subset\>\<frak-g\>> with
    <math|<around|[|X,Y|]>\<in\>I> for all <math|X\<in\>\<frak-g\>> and
    <math|Y\<in\>I> is equal to <math|<around|{|0|}>> or to
    <math|\<frak-g\>>.
  </enumerate>

  <em|What it encodes.> The subspace <math|\<frak-g\>> is determined by
  <math|G>: it is the Lie algebra of the closed matrix group <math|G>, and it
  consists of skew-Hermitian matrices. The group <math|G> is compact.
  Simplicity is simplicity of <math|\<frak-g\>> as a real Lie algebra, with
  the nonabelian condition written out. The embedding
  <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|m|)>> is part of the data: it enters the
  lattice action through the matrix trace and the factor <math|1/m>
  (Definition 1.3).

  <em|Nonstandard features.> (a) <math|G> need not be connected. Any closed
  subgroup of <math|U<around|(|m|)>> whose Lie algebra is simple is allowed,
  for example <math|O<around|(|3|)>\<subset\>U<around|(|3|)>>; only the
  identity component of <math|G> is a compact simple Lie group. (b) The
  theorem is formulated for a group given in a fixed faithful unitary
  representation, not for an abstract compact group.

  <em|Inner product and Casimir constant (used by the construction only).> On
  <math|\<frak-g\>> put <math|<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>>\<assign\>-<tfrac|1|m><math-up|Retr><around|(|X*Y|)>>.
  This is a positive definite, <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant
  inner product. The Casimir constant is

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>\<assign\>-<frac|1|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i><math-up|tr><rsub|\<frak-g\>><around*|(|<math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<around|(|e<rsub|i>|)>> is a
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>>>-orthonormal
  basis of <math|\<frak-g\>>. Equivalently, the Killing form of
  <math|\<frak-g\>> equals <math|-C<rsub|\<frak-g\>><around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>>>.
  Put <math|b<rsub|0>\<assign\>11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|48*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>.
  For <math|G=S*U<around|(|N|)>\<subset\>U<around|(|N|)>> one finds
  <math|C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>=2*N<rsup|2>>. With this inner product the lattice
  coupling <math|\<beta\>> of Definition 1.4 corresponds to
  <math|1/g<rsub|0><rsup|2>> in the formal action
  <math|<frac|1|2*g<rsub|0><rsup|2>>*<big|int><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>><rsup|2>>
  [Assessment, ŸŸ3\U4]. Neither <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>>>
  nor <math|C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>> occurs in the main theorem; only
  <math|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>> does (in the trace test, Definition
  1.16).

  <subsubsection|Spacetime and test functions><label|spacetime-and-test-functions>

  <strong|Definition 1.2 (Spacetime, configurations, test functions).>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Spacetime.> <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> with the Euclidean norm
    <math|<around|\||x|\|>>. For <math|x=<around|(|x<rsup|1>,x<rsup|2>,x<rsup|3>,x<rsup|4>|)>>
    write <math|t<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>x<rsup|4>> (time) and
    <math|<wide|x|\<vect\>>\<assign\><around|(|x<rsup|1>,x<rsup|2>,x<rsup|3>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>.
    The pairing <math|p\<cdot\>b\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>=1><rsup|4>p<rsup|\<mu\>>*b<rsup|\<mu\>>>
    is the Euclidean one. The same <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> serves as
    Euclidean space, for correlation functions, and as Minkowski space, for
    the relativistic theory. In the second role,
    <math|<math-ss|m><around|(|x|)>\<assign\>t<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>-<around|\||<wide|x|\<vect\>>|\|><rsup|2>>
    is the Minkowski square, <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>\<assign\><around|{|x:<around|\||<wide|x|\<vect\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<around|(|x|)>|}>>
    is the closed forward cone, and <math|x> is <em|spacelike> if
    <math|<math-ss|m><around|(|x|)>\<less\>0>.

    <item><em|Configurations.> For <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>> carries the Euclidean norm
    <math|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>
    and Lebesgue measure <math|d*x>. A point is
    <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>> with
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, and <math|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>> is
    the <math|a>-th coordinate of <math|x<rsub|i>>. The set of <em|separated
    configurations> is the open set
  </enumerate>

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Conf><rsub|n>\<assign\><around|{|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>:x<rsub|i>\<neq\>x<rsub|j><text|
    for all >i\<neq\>j|}>.
  </equation*>

  <\enumerate>
    <assign|item-nr|2><item><em|Schwartz functions.>
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<assign\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>;\<bbb-C\>|)>>
    with its Fréchet topology. For <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>,
    <math|<wide|f|\<bar\>>> is the pointwise complex conjugate.

    <item><em|Separated test functions.> <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>
    is the space of <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>>> functions
    <math|f:<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>> whose
    closed support <math|<math-up|supp>f> is compact and contained in
    <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>.
  </enumerate>

  <em|Nonstandard features.> The separated test functions are joint functions
  of all <math|n> points, not products of one-point test functions. They
  vanish in a neighbourhood of every coincidence <math|x<rsub|i>=x<rsub|j>>;
  no statement in the main theorem concerns coincident points.

  <subsubsection|The lattice theory><label|the-lattice-theory>

  <strong|Definition 1.3 (Periodic lattices, link configurations,
  plaquettes).> Let <math|<around|(|m,G,\<frak-g\>|)>> be a gauge group and
  <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1> an integer.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<assign\><around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>
    is the discrete torus with <math|\<ell\>> sites per side, and
    <math|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|4>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>> are
    the unit vectors. A <em|link> is a pair
    <math|<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<times\><around|{|1,2,3,4|}>>;
    it joins <math|y> to <math|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>>.

    <item>A <em|link configuration> is a map
    <math|U:\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<times\><around|{|1,2,3,4|}>\<to\>G>.
    The configuration space <math|G<rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<times\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,4|}>>>
    is compact in the product topology and carries the product of the Borel
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras.

    <item>The <em|plaquette variable> at <math|y> in the plane
    <math|<around|(|\<mu\>,\<nu\>|)>> is
  </enumerate>

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>\<assign\>U<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>*<space|0.17em>U<around|(|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>,\<nu\>|)>*<space|0.17em>U<around|(|y+e<rsub|\<nu\>>,\<mu\>|)><rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>U<around|(|y,\<nu\>|)><rsup|-1>\<in\>G,
  </equation*>

  and the <em|Wilson energy> is

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>><big|sum><rsub|1\<leqslant\>\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>\<leqslant\>4><around*|(|1-<tfrac|1|m><math-up|Retr>U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\enumerate>
    <assign|item-nr|3><item>A <em|gauge transformation>
    <math|\<gamma\>:\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<to\>G> acts by
    <math|<around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\>>|)><around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>\<assign\>\<gamma\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>U<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<gamma\>*<around|(|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>>.

    <item>For <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>/\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\>>,
    <math|r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|j|)>\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> is the unique
    representative of <math|j> in <math|<around|(|-\<ell\>/2,\<ell\>/2|]>>.
  </enumerate>

  <em|What it encodes.> The standard Wilson plaquette action in the
  representation <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|m|)>>, with each unoriented
  plaquette counted once.

  <strong|Definition 1.4 (Wilson measure).> For <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>1>,
  <math|\<beta\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and a Borel probability measure <math|h> on
  <math|G>, the <em|Wilson measure> is the probability measure on
  <math|G<rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<times\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,4|}>>>

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>,h>*<around|(|d*U|)>\<assign\>Z<rsup|-1>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>>h*<around*|(|d*U<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>|)>,<space|2em>Z\<assign\><big|int>e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>>h*<around*|(|d*U<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  (<math|Z> is finite and positive because
  <math|0\<leqslant\>E<rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>12*\<ell\><rsup|4>>.)

  <em|What it encodes.> With the inner product of Definition 1.1,
  <math|\<beta\>=1/g<rsub|0><rsup|2>> is the inverse squared bare coupling
  [Assessment, Ÿ4]. Other conventions differ by factors of <math|m>; for
  example <math|\<beta\>=m*<space|0.17em>\<beta\><rprime|'>> when the energy
  is written <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>*<big|sum><around|(|m-<math-up|Retr>U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)>>
  [Assessment, Ÿ11].

  <label|wilson-family> <strong|Definition 1.5 (Wilson family).> A <em|Wilson
  family> <math|\<cal-W\>> for a gauge group
  <math|<around|(|m,G,\<frak-g\>|)>> consists of:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>strictly increasing sequences of positive integers
    <math|<around|(|L<rsub|p>|)><rsub|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> (physical side
    lengths) and <math|<around|(|D<rsub|N>|)><rsub|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
    (inverse lattice spacings). Put <math|a<rsub|N>\<assign\>1/D<rsub|N>>,
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>\<assign\>L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>> and
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>\<assign\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>>>.
    The <em|physical position> of a site <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>>
    is
  </enumerate>

  <\equation*>
    <wide|y|^>\<assign\>a<rsub|N><around*|(|r<rsub|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>><around|(|y<rsup|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,r<rsub|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>><around|(|y<rsup|4>|)>|)>\<in\><around|(|-L<rsub|p>/2,L<rsub|p>/2|]><rsup|4>;
  </equation*>

  <\enumerate>
    <assign|item-nr|1><item>real numbers <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> for
    <math|N,p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> (bare couplings);

    <item>a Borel probability measure <math|h> on <math|G> that is a Haar
    measure (left-invariant, finite on compact sets, positive on nonempty
    open sets);

    <item>a set <math|\<cal-T\>> (<em|tokens>) and, for all
    <math|N,p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\>> and
    <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>>, a function
    <math|I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y;\<cdot\>|)>:G<rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>\<times\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,4|}>>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>
    (the <em|insertion> of <math|v> at the site <math|y>).
  </enumerate>

  We write <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>\<assign\>\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>,h>>.

  <em|What it encodes.> <math|N> is the ultraviolet index and <math|p> the
  volume index. The torus <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>> with spacing
  <math|a<rsub|N>> has physical side <math|\<ell\><rsub|N,p>*a<rsub|N>=L<rsub|p>>.
  Since <math|G> is compact (and second countable), <math|h> is the
  normalized Haar measure.

  <em|Nonstandard features.> The sequences <math|<around|(|D<rsub|N>|)>>,
  <math|<around|(|L<rsub|p>|)>> and <math|<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|)>>
  are part of what the theorem asserts to exist; they are chosen by the
  construction, and the coupling depends on both <math|N> and <math|p>. The
  token set and the insertions are arbitrary in this definition. Nothing here
  says that they discretize curvature polynomials or that they are
  renormalized; their only constraints are Definition 1.6 and the conclusions
  of the theorem.

  <em|In the construction.> <math|L<rsub|p>> is divisible by <math|4>;
  <math|D<rsub|N>=<big|prod><rsub|k\<less\>N>b<rsub|k>> with odd integers
  <math|b<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>3>; <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> is the bare
  coupling at level <math|N> of a renormalization-group trajectory that
  reaches one fixed reference coupling at level <math|0>, for every <math|N>
  and <math|p>. A token is a pair <math|<around|(|d,c|)>>, where
  <math|c\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|k<rsub|d>>> is a coordinate vector, in a fixed
  basis, of the space <math|V<rsub|d>> of <math|G>-invariant polynomials of
  weight at most <math|d> in the Taylor coefficients of a connection in
  radial gauge. Equivalently, <math|V<rsub|d>> consists of the
  gauge-invariant polynomials in the curvature and its covariant derivatives
  at a point, where the curvature has weight <math|2> and each derivative
  adds <math|1>. The insertion is

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|N,p><around*|(|<around|(|d,c|)>,y;U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*<space|0.17em>P<rsub|d>*<around*|[|B<rsub|N,p,d>*<space|0.17em>c|]><around|(|y;U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|P<rsub|d><around|[|c<rprime|'>|]>> is a fixed polynomial in the
  matrix entries, and their conjugates, of the links inside a fixed finite
  stencil translated to <math|y>. It depends linearly on <math|c<rprime|'>>
  and reproduces the classical label <math|c<rprime|'>> to Taylor order
  <math|d> in the spacing on links obtained by parallel transport of
  polynomial potentials in radial gauge. <math|B<rsub|N,p,d>> is an
  invertible linear map, the renormalization, with no explicit formula for
  its inverse [Assessment, ŸŸ4.1\U4.2 and the glossary entry for the
  insertion].

  <label|regular-wilson-family> <strong|Definition 1.6 (Regular Wilson
  family).> A Wilson family is <em|regular> if the following hold.

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(Reg1) Logarithmic running of the bare coupling.> There is
    <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> such that for every
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> there is <math|N<rsub|0>> with
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    <mid|\|><frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|log <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>>-b<rsub|\<ast\>><mid|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\><space|2em><text|for
    all >N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0><text| and all >p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>.
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(Reg2) Continuity.> For all <math|N,p,v,y> the function
    <math|U\<mapsto\>I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y;U|)>> is continuous.

    <item><strong|(Reg3) Gauge invariance.>
    <math|I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y;U<rsup|\<gamma\>>|)>=I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y;U|)>>
    for all <math|N,p,v,y>, all configurations <math|U> and all gauge
    transformations <math|\<gamma\>:\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>\<to\>G>.

    <item><strong|(Reg4) Fixed finite stencil.> For every
    <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\>> there is a finite set
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|v>\<subset\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> with the following
    property. For all <math|N,p> and <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>>, put
    <math|A\<assign\><around|{|y+<around|(|z<bmod>\<ell\><rsub|N,p>|)>:z\<in\>\<Sigma\><rsub|v>|}>\<subset\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p>>.
    If two configurations <math|U,V> agree on every link
    <math|<around|(|y<rprime|'>,\<mu\>|)>> with <math|y<rprime|'>\<in\>A> and
    <math|y<rprime|'>+e<rsub|\<mu\>>\<in\>A>, then
    <math|I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y;U|)>=I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y;V|)>>.
  </itemize-dot>

  <em|What it encodes.> (Reg1) is the logarithmic growth of the inverse bare
  coupling required by asymptotic freedom, uniformly in the volume.
  (Reg2)\U(Reg4) say that each insertion is a continuous, gauge-invariant
  function of the links in a patch of fixed size in lattice units. Its
  physical diameter is therefore <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|N>|)>>.

  <em|Nonstandard features.> (Reg1) asserts only that <em|some> positive
  slope exists. In the construction <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=2*b<rsub|0>=11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>,
  but this value is not part of the main theorem. The stencil
  <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|v>> is independent of <math|N>, <math|p> and <math|y>.
  Apart from (Reg2)\U(Reg4), nothing restricts how the insertions depend on
  <math|N> and <math|p>.

  <strong|Definition 1.7 (Lattice moments, smeared moments, admissible
  tests).> Let <math|\<cal-W\>> be a Wilson family, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
  and <math|v=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>>.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>For <math|y=<around|(|y<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,y<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p><rsup|n>>,
    the <em|lattice moment> is
  </enumerate>

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>\<assign\><big|int><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|i>,y<rsub|i>;U|)>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|d*U|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\enumerate>
    <assign|item-nr|1><item>For <math|f\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>,
    the <em|smeared moment> is
  </enumerate>

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>\<assign\>a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N,p><rsup|n>>f<around|(|<wide|y|^><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,<wide|y|^><rsub|n>|)>*<space|0.17em>M<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\enumerate>
    <assign|item-nr|2><item><math|f\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>
    is <em|<math|p>-admissible> if <math|<math-up|supp>f\<subset\><around|{|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>:-L<rsub|p>/2\<less\>x<rsub|i><rsup|a>\<less\>L<rsub|p>/2<text|
    for all >i,a|}>>.
  </enumerate>

  <em|What it encodes.> <math|C<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>> is the Riemann
  sum, at spacing <math|a<rsub|N>>, of the test function against the
  <math|n>-point function of the insertions, in the physical units of the
  box. The sum runs over all ordered <math|n>-tuples of sites. A tuple with a
  repeated site is mapped to a non-separated configuration, where <math|f>
  vanishes, so it contributes nothing. For a <math|p>-admissible test,
  <math|<math-up|supp>f> lies in <math|<around*|(|<around|(|-L<rsub|p>/2,L<rsub|p>/2|)><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>,
  and <math|<around|(|-L<rsub|p>/2,L<rsub|p>/2|)><rsup|4>> lies inside the
  fundamental domain <math|<around|(|-L<rsub|p>/2,L<rsub|p>/2|]><rsup|4>> of
  the torus, so no periodic image of <math|<math-up|supp>f> enters.

  <label|ordered-convergence> <strong|Definition 1.8 (Ordered convergence
  along a volume sequence).> Let <math|\<cal-W\>> be a Wilson family, let
  <math|P:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>>, and let
  <math|K=(K<rsub|n>(v;\<cdot\><around|)||)>> be a family of functions
  <math|K<rsub|n><around|(|v;\<cdot\>|)>:<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>,
  indexed by <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>>. We
  say that <em|<math|\<cal-W\>> converges along <math|P> to <math|K>> if for
  every <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, every <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>> and
  every <math|f\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>:

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|f> is <math|P<around|(|k|)>>-admissible for all sufficiently
    large <math|k>; and

    <item>there is a sequence <math|<around|(|R<rsub|p>|)><rsub|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
    of complex numbers such that

    <\itemize-dot>
      <item>(a) for every <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> for which <math|f> is
      <math|p>-admissible, <math|C<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>\<to\>R<rsub|p>>
      as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>; and

      <item>(b) <math|R<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>>\<to\><with|math-display|true|<big|int><rsub|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>K<rsub|n><around|(|v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>>
      as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    </itemize-dot>
  </enumerate>

  <em|What it encodes.> There are two successive limits. First comes the
  ultraviolet limit <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> at fixed physical volume, along
  the full sequence <math|N=0,1,2,\<ldots\>>, for every admissible volume.
  Then comes the infinite-volume limit along <math|P>.

  <em|Nonstandard features.> The infinite-volume limit is taken only along
  the given sequence <math|P>. Nothing is said about diagonal limits
  <math|N=N<around|(|p|)>>, about exchanging the limits, or about rates.
  Condition 1 holds automatically when <math|P> is strictly increasing,
  because <math|L<rsub|p>\<to\>\<infty\>>. The numbers <math|R<rsub|p>> for
  inadmissible <math|p> are unconstrained. Only the restriction of
  <math|K<rsub|n><around|(|v;\<cdot\>|)>> to <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> is
  tested.

  <subsubsection|The relativistic theory><label|the-relativistic-theory>

  <strong|Definition 1.9 (Relativistic field-theory data).> A
  <em|relativistic field-theory datum> <math|\<frak-W\>> consists of the
  following.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>A complex Hilbert space <math|\<cal-H\>> and a complex vector space
    <math|\<cal-L\>> (the <em|labels>).

    <item>A <em|field system>: a complex linear subspace
    <math|D\<subset\>\<cal-H\>> (the <em|domain>), a vector
    <math|\<Omega\>\<in\>D> (the <em|vacuum>), and a map
    <math|<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>\<mapsto\>\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>> from
    <math|\<cal-L\>\<times\>\<cal-S\>> to the complex linear maps
    <math|D\<to\>D>, complex linear in <math|\<ell\>> and in <math|f>.

    <item>A complex antilinear map <math|\<ell\>\<mapsto\>\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>>
    of <math|\<cal-L\>>.

    <item><em|Translations>: bounded operators <math|T<around|(|b|)>>,
    <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, with <math|T<around|(|0|)>=1>,
    <math|T<around|(|b|)>*T<around|(|b<rprime|'>|)>=T*<around|(|b+b<rprime|'>|)>>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<around|(|b|)>*\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    and <math|b\<mapsto\>T<around|(|b|)>*\<psi\>> continuous for every
    <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-H\>>.

    <item>An <em|energy\Umomentum measure>: an assignment of a bounded
    operator <math|E<around|(|A|)>> to subsets
    <math|A\<subset\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, with
    <math|E<around|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=0> and
    <math|E<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>=1>, such that for Borel sets
    <math|E<around|(|A|)>*E<around|(|B|)>=E*<around|(|A\<cap\>B|)>> and
    <math|E<around|(|A|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=E<around|(|A|)>>, and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>E<around|(|A<rsub|i>|)>*\<psi\>=E<around|(|<big|cup><rsub|i>A<rsub|i>|)>*\<psi\>>
    (an unconditionally convergent sum) for pairwise disjoint Borel sets
    <math|A<rsub|i>> and every <math|\<psi\>>. It comes with finite Borel
    measures <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<psi\>>>, <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-H\>>, such
    that <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<psi\>><around|(|A|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,E<around|(|A|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>
    for Borel <math|A>.

    <item><em|Poincaré data>: a set <math|\<cal-P\>> with a distinguished
    element <math|1>, a product <math|<around|(|g,g<rprime|'>|)>\<mapsto\>g*g<rprime|'>>,
    a topology, and a homeomorphism <math|\<kappa\>:\<cal-P\>\<to\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>\<times\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>,
    <math|g\<mapsto\><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|g>,b<rsub|g>|)>>, with
  </enumerate>

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\><around|(|1|)>=<around|(|1,0|)>,<space|2em>\<kappa\>*<around|(|g*g<rprime|'>|)>=<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|g>*\<Lambda\><rsub|g<rprime|'>>,\<Lambda\><rsub|g>*b<rsub|g<rprime|'>>+b<rsub|g>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>> is the group of linear maps
  <math|\<Lambda\>> of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> with <math|det \<Lambda\>=1>,
  <math|<math-ss|m><around|(|\<Lambda\>*x|)>=<math-ss|m><around|(|x|)>> for
  all <math|x>, and <math|\<Lambda\><around|(|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>|)>=<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>,
  with its usual topology. In addition there are: a map
  <math|\<tau\>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<to\>\<cal-P\>> with
  <math|\<kappa\><around|(|\<tau\><around|(|b|)>|)>=<around|(|1,b|)>>,
  <math|\<tau\><around|(|0|)>=1> and <math|\<tau\>*<around|(|b+b<rprime|'>|)>=\<tau\><around|(|b|)>*\<tau\><around|(|b<rprime|'>|)>>;
  for each <math|g\<in\>\<cal-P\>>, a bounded operator <math|U<around|(|g|)>>
  on <math|\<cal-H\>> and a complex linear bijection
  <math|\<rho\><around|(|g|)>> of <math|\<cal-L\>>. These satisfy
  <math|U<around|(|1|)>=1>, <math|U*<around|(|g*g<rprime|'>|)>=U<around|(|g|)>*U<around|(|g<rprime|'>|)>>,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U<around|(|g|)>*\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>>>,
  continuity of <math|g\<mapsto\>U<around|(|g|)>*\<psi\>> for every
  <math|\<psi\>>, and <math|U<around|(|\<tau\><around|(|b|)>|)>=T<around|(|b|)>>.
  The action on test functions is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|g\<cdot\>f|)><around|(|x|)>\<assign\>f*<around*|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|g><rsup|-1>*<around|(|x-b<rsub|g>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  <em|What it encodes.> Through <math|\<kappa\>>, <math|\<cal-P\>> is a
  topological group isomorphic to the proper orthochronous Poincaré group
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>=\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>\<ltimes\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>.
  Then <math|U> is a strongly continuous unitary representation of
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>: each <math|U<around|(|g|)>> is
  isometric and invertible. Its restriction to translations is <math|T>.
  <math|E> is a projection-valued measure on the Borel sets of
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>, with diagonal measures
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<psi\>>>. The map <math|\<rho\>> is required to be a
  representation only in (W2) below.

  <label|wightman-axioms> <strong|Definition 1.10 (Wightman axioms).> A
  relativistic field-theory datum satisfies the <em|Wightman axioms> if
  (W0)\U(W3) hold.

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(W0) Spectrum and vacuum.>

    <\itemize-dot>
      <item>(W0a) For all <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-H\>> and
      <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>: <math|<with|math-display|true|<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,T<around|(|b|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>e<rsup|<space|0.17em>i*<space|0.17em>p\<cdot\>b>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|\<psi\>>*<around|(|d*p|)>>>.

      <item>(W0b) <math|E*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<setminus\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>|)>=0>.

      <item>(W0c) If <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-H\>> satisfies
      <math|T<around|(|b|)>*\<psi\>=\<psi\>> for all <math|b>, then
      <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>*<space|0.17em>\<Omega\>>.
    </itemize-dot>

    <item><strong|(W1) Domain, temperedness, adjoint, cyclicity.>

    <\itemize-dot>
      <item>(W1a) <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>>=1>.

      <item>(W1b) <math|D> is dense in <math|\<cal-H\>>.

      <item>(W1c) <math|D=<math-up|span><rsub|\<bbb-C\>><around|{|\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|1>,f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|k>,f<rsub|k>|)>*\<Omega\>:k\<geqslant\>0,\<ell\><rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-L\>,f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>>,
      where the empty product gives <math|\<Omega\>>.

      <item>(W1d) For each <math|\<ell\>\<in\>\<cal-L\>> and
      <math|\<psi\>\<in\>D>, the map <math|\<cal-S\>\<to\>\<cal-H\>>,
      <math|f\<mapsto\>\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<psi\>>, is continuous.

      <item>(W1e) <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<psi\>,\<chi\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*\<chi\>|\<rangle\>>>
      for all <math|\<ell\>\<in\>\<cal-L\>>, <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
      <math|\<psi\>,\<chi\>\<in\>D>.
    </itemize-dot>

    <item><strong|(W2) Poincaré covariance.>

    <\itemize-dot>
      <item>(W2a) <math|\<rho\><around|(|1|)>=<math-up|id>> and
      <math|\<rho\>*<around|(|g*g<rprime|'>|)>=\<rho\><around|(|g|)>*\<rho\><around|(|g<rprime|'>|)>>.

      <item>(W2b) <math|U<around|(|g|)>*D\<subset\>D> for every <math|g>.

      <item>(W2c) <math|U<around|(|g|)>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>> for every
      <math|g>.

      <item>(W2d) <math|U<around|(|g|)>*\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<psi\>=\<Phi\><around|(|\<rho\><around|(|g|)>*\<ell\>,g\<cdot\>f|)>*<space|0.17em>U<around|(|g|)>*\<psi\>>
      for all <math|g,\<ell\>,f> and <math|\<psi\>\<in\>D>.
    </itemize-dot>

    <item><strong|(W3) Locality.> If <math|<math-ss|m><around|(|x-y|)>\<less\>0>
    for all <math|x\<in\><math-up|supp>f> and
    <math|y\<in\><math-up|supp>f<rprime|'>>, then
    <math|\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\><rprime|'>,f<rprime|'>|)>*\<psi\>=\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\><rprime|'>,f<rprime|'>|)>*\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<psi\>>
    for all <math|\<ell\>,\<ell\><rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-L\>> and
    <math|\<psi\>\<in\>D>.
  </itemize-dot>

  <em|What it encodes.> The Gårding\UWightman axioms for a family of bosonic
  fields labelled by <math|\<cal-L\>>, in the following form.

  <em|Nonstandard features.>

  <\enumerate>
    <item>(W0a) uses the Euclidean pairing <math|p\<cdot\>b>. Writing
    <math|P<rsup|0>\<assign\>p<rsup|4>> and
    <math|<wide|P|\<vect\>>\<assign\>-<wide|p|\<vect\>>> gives the usual
    <math|T<around|(|b|)>=e<rsup|i*<around|(|P<rsup|0>*b<rsup|4>-<wide|P|\<vect\>>\<cdot\><wide|b|\<vect\>>|)>>>.
    Since <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>> is invariant under
    <math|<wide|p|\<vect\>>\<mapsto\>-<wide|p|\<vect\>>>, (W0b) is the usual
    spectrum condition. With these conventions the Wightman functions are
    boundary values from the tube where the imaginary parts of the times
    increase from left to right, as in (R13)\U(R15) below.

    <item>The invariance of <math|\<Omega\>> is stated in (W2c); (W0c) is
    uniqueness among translation-invariant vectors.

    <item>(W1c) is an equality: the domain <em|is> the span of the field
    monomials applied to <math|\<Omega\>>. (W1d) is continuity into the norm
    topology, which is stronger than the usual weak temperedness.

    <item>The labels form an abstract complex vector space, possibly
    infinite-dimensional. Nothing requires <math|\<rho\>> to be
    finite-dimensional on any subspace, to be continuous, or to be trivial on
    translations, and <math|\<ell\>\<mapsto\>\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>> need not
    be an involution.

    <item>The symmetry group is <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>
    itself, not its double cover, and all fields commute at spacelike
    separation.
  </enumerate>

  <subsubsection|The continuum hierarchy><label|the-continuum-hierarchy>

  <label|continuum-hierarchy> <strong|Definition 1.11 (Hierarchy over a
  Wilson family).> Let <math|\<cal-W\>> be a Wilson family for
  <math|<around|(|m,G,\<frak-g\>|)>>, with token set <math|\<cal-T\>>. A
  <em|hierarchy over <math|\<cal-W\>>> consists of:

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(H1)> a relativistic field-theory datum
    <math|\<frak-W\>=<around|(|\<cal-H\>,\<cal-L\>,D,\<Omega\>,\<Phi\>,<rsup|\<ast\>>,T,E,\<cal-P\>,\<kappa\>,\<tau\>,U,\<rho\>|)>>;

    <item><strong|(H2)> for each <math|d\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a
    finite-dimensional complex normed space <math|F<rsub|d>> (the <em|fields
    of degree <math|d>>) and a complex linear map
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-L\>>. Write
    <math|\<bbb-F\>\<assign\><big|sqcup><rsub|d>F<rsub|d>> for the set of
    pairs <math|a=<around|(|d,\<varphi\>|)>> with
    <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>F<rsub|d>>, and put <math|deg a\<assign\>d> and
    <math|\<iota\><around|(|a|)>\<assign\>\<iota\><rsub|d><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>;

    <item><strong|(H3)> a map <math|\<pi\>:\<cal-T\>\<to\>\<bbb-F\>> (the
    <em|projection of tokens to fields>);

    <item><strong|(H4)> a compact metric space <math|X> (the <em|phases>)
    with its Borel <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, a Borel measure <math|\<nu\>> on
    <math|X>, and a point <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>X> (the
    <em|selected phase>);

    <item><strong|(H5)> for each <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and each <em|state>
    <math|s\<in\>X\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>, a function
    <math|S<rsup|s>:\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>\<times\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>,
    <math|<around|(|a;x|)>\<mapsto\>S<rsup|s><around|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|n>;x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>
    (the <em|correlation functions>; <math|s=<math-up|mix>> is the
    unselected, or mixed, state);

    <item><strong|(H6)> for each <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a function
    <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n>:\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>\<times\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|n>\<times\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>|)><rsup|n>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>,
    <math|<around|(|a;z,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>\<mapsto\><math-ss|F><rsub|n><around|(|a;z,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>>
    (the <em|continuation>);

    <item><strong|(H7)> OPE data: for each <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a finite
    set <math|<math-up|Ch><rsub|j>> (channels) and elements
    <math|O<rsub|j,\<kappa\>>\<in\>F<rsub|j>> for
    <math|\<kappa\>\<in\><math-up|Ch><rsub|j>>; and, for each finite set
    <math|I>, each <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|I>>, each <math|j> and each
    <math|\<kappa\>\<in\><math-up|Ch><rsub|j>>, a function
    <math|c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>><around|(|a;\<cdot\>,\<cdot\>|)>:<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|I>\<times\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>,
    <math|<around|(|x,o|)>\<mapsto\>c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>><around|(|a;x,o|)>>
    (the <em|OPE coefficients>);

    <item><strong|(H8)> an element <math|\<Theta\>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> (the
    <em|centered trace>).
  </itemize-dot>

  The field <math|\<pi\><around|(|v|)>> is the continuum counterpart of the
  token <math|v>. A token <math|v> with <math|\<pi\><around|(|v|)>=a> is a
  <em|lattice representative> of <math|a>.

  <em|What it encodes.> <math|F<rsub|d>> is the space of local fields of
  degree <math|d>. <math|\<cal-L\>> labels the relativistic fields, and
  <math|\<iota\>> sends a field to the label of its relativistic counterpart.
  <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>> is the correlation function produced by the
  lattice (property (P1)); the <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> are the phases into
  which it decomposes; <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n>> is the analytic
  continuation of the selected phase.

  <em|Nonstandard features.> Everything in a hierarchy is abstract data; all
  constraints come from Definitions 1.12\U1.17. The degree <math|d> is an
  index that the OPE bounds use as a weight. It is not required to be a
  scaling dimension. Nothing requires <math|F<rsub|d>\<subset\>F<rsub|d+1>>,
  compatibility of the maps <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>> for different <math|d>,
  or any relation of <math|F<rsub|d>> to curvature polynomials. The norms on
  <math|F<rsub|d>> play no role. The correlation functions are functions on
  all of <math|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>, but only their values
  on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> are constrained.

  <em|In the construction.> <math|F<rsub|d>=V<rsub|d>/N<rsub|d>>, where
  <math|V<rsub|d>\<cong\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|k<rsub|d>>> is the token coefficient
  space of degree <math|d> and <math|N<rsub|d>> consists of the <math|c> such
  that <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><around|(|c<text| at
  >x<rsub|0>,b<rsub|1><text| at >x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|k><text| at
  >x<rsub|k>|)>=0> for all <math|k>, all tokens <math|b<rsub|i>> and all
  separated points. <math|\<cal-L\>> is the linear span of the "response"
  functions <math|R<rsub|c>:<around|(|k,b,x|)>\<mapsto\>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><around|(|c,b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|k>;x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|k>|)>>.
  Then <math|\<iota\><rsub|d><around|(|<around|[|c|]>|)>=R<rsub|c>> and
  <math|\<pi\><around|(|d,c|)>=<around|(|d,<around|[|c|]>|)>>. The
  construction also proves that <math|F<rsub|d>> is linearly isomorphic to
  the space of gauge-invariant polynomials of weight <math|\<leqslant\>d> in
  a free curvature satisfying the source-free Maxwell equations and the
  Bianchi identities, and that every phase has the same null spaces
  [Assessment, Ÿ3].

  <em|Phase space.> The phase space <math|X> is the Gelfand spectrum of a
  commutative <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-algebra of time-translated field words
  compressed to the zero-energy subspace of the Osterwalder\USchrader space
  of <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>>. The measure <math|\<nu\>> represents the
  mixed vacuum.

  <em|Phase selection.> The point <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is chosen, by
  an existence argument, among the phases whose ground-state space is
  one-dimensional, which form a set of full <math|\<nu\>>-measure; no other
  condition is imposed, and the trace law below is proved for the phase so
  chosen (<hlink|<verbatim|YM/Org/Phase.lean:60>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Org/Phase.lean#L60-L77>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Phase/Unique.lean:111>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Phase/Unique.lean#L111>).
  The source also contains an alternative selection, not used by the main
  theorem, which additionally requires a condition on the two-point function
  of the trace of the stress tensor along a fixed sequence of scales
  (<hlink|<verbatim|YM/Trace/Revel.lean>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Trace/Revel.lean>);
  the description in [Assessment, glossary entry for the selected phase]
  matches that alternative.

  <em|Continuation and OPE data.> <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n>> is the analytic
  continuation of <math|S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>. The channels index a
  conjugation-invariant basis of a complement of the lower-degree fields in
  <math|F<rsub|j>> [Assessment, glossary entries for the OPE data].

  <label|reconstruction-properties> <strong|Definition 1.12 (Reconstruction
  properties).> A hierarchy has the <em|reconstruction properties> if
  (R1)\U(R15) hold. Here <math|s> ranges over
  <math|X\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>, <math|n> over
  <math|\<bbb-N\>>, and <math|a> over <math|\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>>.

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(R1)> <math|\<frak-W\>> satisfies the Wightman axioms
    (W0)\U(W3).

    <item><strong|(R2) Faithful fields.> For every
    <math|\<ell\>\<in\>\<cal-L\>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>> there is
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with <math|\<Phi\><around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>\<neq\>0>.

    <item><strong|(R3)> Each <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-L\>>
    is injective.

    <item><strong|(R4)> There are <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-F\>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with <math|\<Phi\><around|(|\<iota\><around|(|a|)>,f|)>\<neq\>0>.

    <item><strong|(R5)> Every <math|\<ell\>\<in\>\<cal-L\>> is of the form
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> for some
    <math|d\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>F<rsub|d>>.

    <item><strong|(R6)> <math|\<pi\>:\<cal-T\>\<to\>\<bbb-F\>> is surjective:
    every field has a lattice representative.

    <item><strong|(R7)> <math|\<nu\><around|(|X|)>=1>.

    <item><strong|(R8)> <math|\<nu\><around|(|O|)>\<gtr\>0> for every
    nonempty open set <math|O\<subset\>X>.

    <item><strong|(R9) Permutation symmetry.> For all <math|s>, <math|n>,
    <math|a>, <math|x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> and permutations
    <math|\<sigma\>> of <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>:
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    S<rsup|s><around|(|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|1|)>>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|n|)>>;x<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|1|)>>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|n|)>>|)>=S<rsup|s><around|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|n>;x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(R10) Smoothness.> For all <math|s>, <math|n>, <math|a>,
    the function <math|x\<mapsto\>S<rsup|s><around|(|a;x|)>> is
    <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>>> on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>.

    <item><strong|(R11) Continuity in the phase.> For all <math|n>, <math|a>
    and <math|x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>, the function
    <math|\<xi\>\<mapsto\>S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;x|)>> is continuous on
    <math|X>.

    <item><strong|(R12) Phase decomposition.> For all <math|n>, <math|a> and
    <math|x\<in\><math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>:
    <math|<with|math-display|true|<big|int><rsub|X>S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;x|)>*<space|0.17em>\<nu\>*<around|(|d*\<xi\>|)>=S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><around|(|a;x|)>>>.

    <item><strong|(R13) Analyticity in the times.> For all <math|n>, <math|a>
    and <math|<wide|x|\<vect\>>\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>|)><rsup|n>>,
    the function <math|z\<mapsto\><math-ss|F><rsub|n><around|(|a;z,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>>
    is holomorphic on the ordered tube
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Z\><rsub|n>\<assign\><around|{|z\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|n>:<math-up|Im>z<rsub|1>\<less\><math-up|Im>z<rsub|2>\<less\>\<ldots\>\<less\><math-up|Im>z<rsub|n>|}>.
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(R14) Euclidean points.> For all <math|n>, <math|a> and
    <math|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>> with
    <math|t<around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<ldots\>\<less\>t<around|(|x<rsub|n>|)>>:
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|F><rsub|n><around*|(|a;<around|(|i*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,i*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|x<rsub|n>|)>|)>,<around|(|<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|n>|)>|)>=S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|a;x|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(R15) Boundary values.> For all <math|n>, <math|a>, real
    numbers <math|h<rsub|1>\<less\>\<ldots\>\<less\>h<rsub|n>> and
    <math|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, as
    <math|r\<to\>0<rsup|+>>,
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>><math-ss|F><rsub|n><around*|(|a;<around|(|t<around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>+i*<space|0.17em>r*<space|0.17em>h<rsub|k>|)><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>,<around|(|<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|k>|)><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|k><around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x\<longrightarrow\><around*|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><around|(|\<iota\><around|(|a<rsub|1>|)>,f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<Phi\><around|(|\<iota\><around|(|a<rsub|n>|)>,f<rsub|n>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  <em|What it encodes.> (R1)\U(R6): a Wightman theory whose fields are
  labelled by the continuum fields; every nonzero field acts nontrivially;
  every field comes from the lattice. (R7)\U(R12): a decomposition of the
  unselected correlations into continuous phases. (R13)\U(R15): the
  relativistic theory is the one obtained by analytic continuation of the
  selected phase: its vacuum expectation values are boundary values of a
  function holomorphic in the complex times that agrees with
  <math|S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>> at purely imaginary times.

  <em|Remarks.>

  <\enumerate>
    <item>Only the selected phase <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> carries a
    relativistic theory in the main theorem. Other phases enter only through
    (R9)\U(R12).

    <item>For fixed <math|<wide|x|\<vect\>>>, the purely imaginary points
    <math|<around|{|z\<in\>\<cal-Z\><rsub|n>:<math-up|Re>z=0|}>> form a
    totally real submanifold of maximal dimension in the connected open set
    <math|\<cal-Z\><rsub|n>>. By the identity theorem, (R13) and (R14)
    determine <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n><around|(|a;\<cdot\>,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>>
    from <math|S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>; through (R15) the vacuum
    expectation values of <math|\<frak-W\>> on product test functions are
    then determined by <math|S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>. (This is a
    consequence, not a separate clause.)

    <item>(R4) prevents (R2) from holding vacuously, for instance with
    <math|\<cal-L\>=<around|{|0|}>>.

    <item>In (R15) the formal statement does not separately assert that the
    integrand is integrable for small <math|r\<gtr\>0>. In (P1) and (R12)
    integrability follows from (R10) and from (R11) with (R7), respectively.

    <item>No Euclidean covariance, reflection positivity or cluster property
    of any <math|S<rsup|s>> is asserted.
  </enumerate>

  <label|operator-product-expansion> <strong|Definition 1.13 (Operator
  product expansion).> Let a hierarchy be given.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>For <math|r\<gtr\>0>, <math|M\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, a finite set <math|I>,
    <math|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|I>> and
    <math|o\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>>: <math|<around|(|x,o|)>> is an
    <em|<math|<around|(|r,M,\<eta\>|)>>-cluster> if
    <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-o|\|>\<leqslant\>M*r> for all <math|i> and
    <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*r>
    for all <math|i\<neq\>i<rprime|'>>.

    <item>For <math|c\<gtr\>0>, a finite set <math|J> and
    <math|y\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|J>>: <math|y> is
    <em|<math|c>-clear of <math|o>> if <math|<around|\||o-y<rsub|j>|\|>\<geqslant\>c>
    for all <math|j> and <math|<around|\||y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|j<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>c>
    for all <math|j\<neq\>j<rprime|'>>.

    <item>For <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|I>>, the <em|total degree> is
    <math|w<around|(|a|)>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>deg a<rsub|i>>.

    <item>For <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|I>>,
    <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|J>>, <math|x\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|I>>
    and <math|y\<in\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|J>>,
    <math|S<rsup|s><around|(|a,x;b,y|)>> is the
    <math|<around|(|<around|\||I|\|>+<around|\||J|\|>|)>>-point function
    <math|S<rsup|s>> evaluated on the concatenated lists of fields and
    points, enumerated in one fixed order of <math|I\<sqcup\>J>.
  </enumerate>

  The hierarchy has the <em|OPE property> if:

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(O1) Coefficient bounds.> For every finite set <math|I>,
    <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|I>>, <math|M\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|\<kappa\>\<in\><math-up|Ch><rsub|j>>,
    for all sufficiently small <math|r\<gtr\>0> and every
    <math|<around|(|r,M,\<eta\>|)>>-cluster <math|<around|(|x,o|)>>:
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>><around|(|a;x,o|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|<space|0.17em>j-w<around|(|a|)>-\<varepsilon\>>.
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(O2) Remainder bounds.> For all finite sets <math|I,J>,
    <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|I>>, <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|J>>,
    <math|M\<geqslant\>0>, <math|\<eta\>,c,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|D\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, for all sufficiently small <math|r\<gtr\>0>,
    every <math|<around|(|r,M,\<eta\>|)>>-cluster <math|<around|(|x,o|)>>,
    every <math|y> that is <math|c>-clear of <math|o>, and every state
    <math|s\<in\>X\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>:
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    <mid|\|>S<rsup|s><around|(|a,x;b,y|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|D><big|sum><rsub|\<kappa\>\<in\><math-up|Ch><rsub|j>>c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>><around|(|a;x,o|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|s><around*|(|<around|(|j,O<rsub|j,\<kappa\>>|)>,o;b,y|)><mid|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|<space|0.17em>D+1-w<around|(|a|)>-\<varepsilon\>>.
  </equation*>

  <em|What it encodes.> A cluster of fields <math|a> at points <math|x> of
  size <math|O<around|(|r|)>> around <math|o> is replaced, inside any
  correlation with distant spectators <math|b>, by the finite sum of single
  fields <math|O<rsub|j,\<kappa\>>> of degree <math|j\<leqslant\>D> at
  <math|o>, up to an error <math|O<around|(|r<rsup|D+1-w<around|(|a|)>-\<varepsilon\>>|)>>.

  <em|Nonstandard features.> One system of coefficients serves every
  spectator list <math|b>, every truncation degree <math|D> and every state
  <math|s>, the mixed one and every phase. The threshold <math|\<delta\>> in
  "sufficiently small <math|r>" depends on
  <math|<around|(|I,J,a,b,M,\<eta\>,c,\<varepsilon\>,D|)>> in (O2), and on
  <math|<around|(|I,a,M,\<eta\>,\<varepsilon\>,j,\<kappa\>|)>> in (O1). It is
  uniform in <math|s>, <math|x>, <math|o> and <math|y>. For
  <math|M*r\<less\>c> every configuration that occurs is separated, so the
  OPE concerns separated configurations only. The degree <math|w> is the sum
  of the degree indices of Definition 1.11, not a sum of scaling dimensions;
  the loss <math|\<varepsilon\>> leaves room for logarithms. The statement is
  a finite-truncation asymptotic bound. Convergence as
  <math|D\<to\>\<infty\>>, uniqueness of the coefficients and their relation
  to perturbation theory are not asserted.

  <label|centered-trace> <strong|Definition 1.14 (Centered trace, stress
  tensor, time-axis correlation).> For a hierarchy, the <em|time-axis trace
  correlation> is

  <\equation*>
    G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>\<assign\>S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around*|(|<around|(|4,\<Theta\>|)>,<around|(|4,\<Theta\>|)>;<around|(|<wide|0|\<vect\>>,-r/2|)>,<around|(|<wide|0|\<vect\>>,r/2|)>|)>,<space|2em>r\<in\>\<bbb-R\>,
  </equation*>

  the selected-phase two-point function of <math|\<Theta\>> at two points of
  the time axis, at times <math|-r/2> and <math|r/2>.

  <em|In the main theorem>, <math|\<Theta\>> is an arbitrary element of
  <math|F<rsub|4>>, constrained only by the trace test (Definition 1.16). The
  main theorem contains no stress tensor.

  <em|In the construction.> The <em|stress tensor> is a family
  <math|T=<around|(|T<rsub|a*b>|)><rsub|a,b=1><rsup|4>> of fields
  <math|T<rsub|a*b>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> with the following properties:

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item>it is symmetric, <math|T<rsub|a*b>=T<rsub|b*a>>, and real (fixed by
    the field conjugation of <math|F<rsub|4>>);

    <item>it is conserved, <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*T<rsub|a*b>=0>
    in <math|F<rsub|5>>, where <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>>
    is the field derivative;

    <item>it transforms as a rank-two tensor under <math|O<around|(|4|)>>;

    <item>it satisfies Ward identities in the mixed state and in every phase.
    For a field <math|B\<in\>F<rsub|d>> at <math|x<rsub|0>>, spectators at
    <math|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>> (all points distinct), and a real
    <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)>>
    equal to <math|1> near <math|x<rsub|0>> and vanishing near
    <math|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>>,
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    -<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>S<around*|(|T<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>,B<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y=<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*x<rsub|0><rsup|b>>*S<around*|(|B<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and similarly for rotations.

  The symmetric tensors with entries in <math|F<rsub|4>> that are real,
  centered with respect to <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>>, transform as
  rank-two tensors under signed permutations of the coordinates, and have
  vanishing divergence in <math|F<rsub|5>> form a one-dimensional real space;
  the Ward identity fixes the normalization [Assessment, Ÿ7.1]. The
  constructed centered trace is

  <\equation*>
    \<Theta\>=<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>-S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<b-1\><rsub|4>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>> is the
  selected one-point function, which is constant in the point, and
  <math|\<b-1\><rsub|4>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> is the unit field, whose one-point
  function is <math|1> [Assessment, Ÿ7.2]. The detailed theorem (Remark 2.4)
  identifies the operators: <math|\<Phi\><around|(|\<iota\><around|(|4,\<Theta\>|)>,f|)>=\<Phi\><around*|(|\<iota\><around|(|4,<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>|)>,f|)>-S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>|)><around*|(|<big|int>f|)><space|0.17em><math-up|id>>.

  <label|wick-profiles> <strong|Definition 1.15 (Mass-shell kernels and Wick
  profiles).> A <em|mode> is a quadruple <math|<around|(|<math-ss|m>,j,q,h|)>>
  with <math|<math-ss|m>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|j,q\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, and
  <math|h:\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> continuous with
  <math|h*<around|(|\<lambda\>*p|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|j>*h<around|(|p|)>> for
  all <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>. With
  <math|E<rsub|<math-ss|m>><around|(|p|)>\<assign\><sqrt|<math-ss|m><rsup|2>+<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>>,
  its <em|kernel> is

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|<math-ss|m>,q,h><around|(|r|)>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>><frac|E<rsub|<math-ss|m>><around|(|p|)><rsup|q>*<space|0.17em>h<around|(|p|)>|2*E<rsub|<math-ss|m>><around|(|p|)>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-r*E<rsub|<math-ss|m>><around|(|p|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*p.
  </equation*>

  A <em|Wick profile> is a function of the form

  <\equation*>
    W<around|(|r|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>=1><rsup|A>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<big|prod><rsub|\<beta\>=1><rsup|n<rsub|\<alpha\>>>K<rsub|<math-ss|m><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>,q<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>,h<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>><around|(|r|)>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|A,n<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
  <math|c<rsub|\<alpha\>>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> and modes
  <math|<around|(|<math-ss|m><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>,j<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>,q<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>,h<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>|)>>.
  An empty product equals <math|1>.

  <em|What it encodes.> For <math|r\<gtr\>0> every integral converges
  absolutely. <math|K<rsub|<math-ss|m>,q,h><around|(|r|)>> has the form of a
  two-point function of a free field of mass <math|<math-ss|m>> at Euclidean
  time separation <math|r> and zero spatial separation, with <math|q> time
  derivatives and a homogeneous momentum numerator for spatial derivatives
  (normalizations are absorbed in <math|h> and <math|c<rsub|\<alpha\>>>).
  Products of kernels correspond to Wick contractions. Thus Wick profiles
  include the time-axis two-point functions of finite Wick polynomials in
  finitely many free fields with finitely many masses [Assessment, Ÿ7.3].

  <label|trace-test> <strong|Definition 1.16 (Trace test).> A hierarchy over
  a Wilson family for <math|<around|(|m,G,\<frak-g\>|)>> <em|passes the trace
  test> if:

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(T1)> <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><around|(|\<iota\><around|(|4,\<Theta\>|)>,f|)>*<space|0.17em>\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0>
    for every <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>;

    <item><strong|(T2)> <math|3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>/\<pi\><rsup|4>\<gtr\>0>;

    <item><strong|(T3)> <math|<with|math-display|true|r<rsup|8><around*|(|log
    <around|(|1/r|)>|)><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>\<longrightarrow\><frac|3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>>> as <math|r\<to\>0<rsup|+>> (a limit in
    <math|\<bbb-C\>>);

    <item><strong|(T4)> for every Wick profile <math|W> and every
    <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> there is <math|r\<in\><around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>
    with <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>\<neq\>W<around|(|r|)>>.
  </itemize-dot>

  <em|What it encodes.> <math|\<Theta\>> has vanishing vacuum expectation in
  <math|\<frak-W\>> (T1). Its selected two-point function on the time axis
  behaves like <math|<frac|3*dim \<frak-g\>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>*<space|0.17em>r<rsup|-8>*<around|(|log
  1/r|)><rsup|-2>> (T3). The factor <math|<around|(|log 1/r|)><rsup|-2>> is
  the signature of a trace generated by renormalization, the trace anomaly,
  with one-loop running; the constant agrees with the perturbative value
  [Assessment, Ÿ7.2]. No Wick profile agrees with <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> on
  any interval <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> (T4). Here
  <math|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>> is the real dimension of
  <math|\<frak-g\>\<subset\>M<rsub|m><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>>.

  <em|Nonstandard features.> (T2) holds for every simple <math|\<frak-g\>>;
  it records that the constant in (T3) is positive. All four clauses concern
  the selected phase, a single field <math|\<Theta\>>, and in (T3)\U(T4) a
  single direction (the time axis). (T4) excludes only the specified finite
  class of profiles, and only for this one function.

  <subsubsection|The properties along a volume
  sequence><label|the-properties-along-a-volume-sequence>

  <strong|Definition 1.17 (Main properties).> Let <math|\<cal-W\>> be a
  Wilson family and <math|P:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>>. A hierarchy over
  <math|\<cal-W\>> has the <em|main properties along <math|P>> if:

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(P1) Lattice origin.> <math|\<cal-W\>> converges along
    <math|P> (Definition 1.8) to the kernels
  </itemize-dot>

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|n><around|(|v;x|)>\<assign\>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><around*|(|\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>;x|)>,<space|2em>v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>;
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(P2) Reconstruction.> (R1)\U(R15) hold;

    <item><strong|(P3) Operator product expansion.> (O1)\U(O2) hold;

    <item><strong|(P4) Trace test.> (T1)\U(T4) hold.
  </itemize-dot>

  <subsection|2. The main theorem><label|2-the-main-theorem>

  <label|public-theorem> <strong|Theorem 2.1 (Main theorem).> Let
  <math|<around|(|m,G,\<frak-g\>|)>> be a gauge group with <math|\<frak-g\>>
  simple. Then there is a regular Wilson family <math|\<cal-W\>> for <math|G>
  with the following property. For every strictly increasing sequence
  <math|p:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>> there are a strictly increasing sequence
  <math|q:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>> and a hierarchy over <math|\<cal-W\>>
  that has the main properties along the volume sequence
  <math|k\<mapsto\>p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>.

  Written out, with the quantifiers in their formal order, the theorem reads
  as follows.

  <\quotation>
    For every integer <math|m\<geqslant\>1>, every closed subgroup
    <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|m|)>> and every real subspace
    <math|\<frak-g\>\<subset\>M<rsub|m><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>> with
    <math|\<frak-g\>=<around|{|X:exp <around|(|s*X|)>\<in\>G\<forall\>s\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>
    and <math|\<frak-g\>> simple,

    <strong|there exist> strictly increasing sequences
    <math|<around|(|L<rsub|p>|)>> and <math|<around|(|D<rsub|N>|)>> of
    positive integers, real numbers <math|<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|)>>,
    the normalized Haar measure <math|h>, a set <math|\<cal-T\>> and
    insertions <math|(I<rsub|N,p>(v,y;\<cdot\><around|)||)>> satisfying
    (Reg1)\U(Reg4), such that

    <strong|for every> strictly increasing <math|p:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>>

    <strong|there exist> a strictly increasing
    <math|q:\<bbb-N\>\<to\>\<bbb-N\>>; a relativistic field-theory datum
    <math|<around|(|\<cal-H\>,\<cal-L\>,D,\<Omega\>,\<Phi\>,<rsup|\<ast\>>,T,E,\<cal-P\>,\<kappa\>,\<tau\>,U,\<rho\>|)>>;
    finite-dimensional spaces <math|F<rsub|d>> with linear maps
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-L\>>; a map
    <math|\<pi\>:\<cal-T\>\<to\>\<bbb-F\>>; a compact metric space <math|X>
    with a Borel measure <math|\<nu\>> and a point
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>; correlation functions <math|S<rsup|s>> for
    <math|s\<in\>X\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>; continuations
    <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n>>; OPE data <math|<around|(|<math-up|Ch><rsub|j>,O<rsub|j,\<kappa\>>,c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>>|)>>;
    and <math|\<Theta\>\<in\>F<rsub|4>>, such that:

    <\itemize-dot>
      <item>(P1) for every <math|n>, every <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>>
      and every <math|f\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>,
      <math|f> is <math|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>-admissible for all
      large <math|k>, and there are numbers <math|R<rsub|p>> with
      <math|C<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>\<to\>R<rsub|p>> as
      <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> for every <math|p> for which <math|f> is
      <math|p>-admissible, and
    </itemize-dot>

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>\<longrightarrow\><big|int><rsub|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><around*|(|\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x*<space|2em><around|(|k\<to\>\<infty\>|)>;
    </equation*>

    <\itemize-dot>
      <item>(P2) (R1)\U(R15); (P3) (O1)\U(O2); (P4) (T1)\U(T4).
    </itemize-dot>
  </quotation>

  <strong|Remark 2.2 (Order of choices).> The lattice data
  <math|<around|(|L<rsub|p>|)>>, <math|<around|(|D<rsub|N>|)>>,
  <math|<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|)>>, <math|\<cal-T\>> and
  <math|<around|(|I<rsub|N,p>|)>> are fixed before the volume sequence
  <math|p>. The refinement <math|q> and the entire continuum structure (the
  fields <math|F<rsub|d>>, the phases, the selected phase, the relativistic
  theory, the OPE data and <math|\<Theta\>>) are chosen after <math|p> and
  may depend on <math|p>. A single hierarchy satisfies (P1)\U(P4)
  simultaneously. Hence the correlation functions to which the lattice
  moments converge are the ones whose phases are decomposed, whose selected
  phase is continued to the relativistic theory, which obey the OPE
  (spectators included), and whose trace correlation is tested.

  <strong|Remark 2.3 (What the lattice determines).> For a fixed volume
  sequence <math|P=p\<circ\>q> and every token list <math|v>, (P1) and (R10)
  determine the function <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><around|(|\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>;\<cdot\>|)>>
  on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> from <math|\<cal-W\>> and <math|P> alone:
  a function continuous on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>> is determined there
  by its integrals against <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>.
  By (R6) every list of fields is of the form
  <math|<around|(|\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<pi\><around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>|)>>.
  The theorem does not claim that the other data of the hierarchy (the phase
  decomposition, the selected phase, the relativistic theory, the OPE
  coefficients, <math|\<Theta\>>) are unique.

  <label|detailed-theorem> <strong|Remark 2.4 (The detailed theorem).> The
  formal development proves Theorem 2.1 as a projection of a longer theorem.
  It has the following form. For every gauge group with <math|\<frak-g\>>
  simple and <strong|every real <math|b\<gtr\>0>>, there is one internal
  construction, a renormalization-group family, such that: its block-spin
  kernel has penalty parameter <math|b> (D16); the internal construction
  predicate holds (D3); the associated Wilson family is regular; and for
  every <em|extraction order> <math|p=<around|(|p<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>,p<rsub|<math-up|vol>>|)>>,
  a pair of strictly increasing sequences, there are a refinement <math|q>, a
  limit correlation table, a final witness (stress tensor, phases,
  continuations, relativistic theory), relativistic theories for all phases,
  an OPE reader, contact devices and a scaling sequence, such that (D1), (D2)
  and (D4)\U(D15) below hold. (D3) and (D16) are stated once, before the
  quantifier over <math|p>. Theorem 2.1 is the case <math|b=1>,
  <math|p<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>=<math-up|id>>, <math|p<rsub|<math-up|vol>>=p>;
  the public <math|q> is the volume component of the refinement. The extra
  conclusions are as follows. Items (D3) and (D5)\U(D15) are summarized from
  their definitions and the source comments; not all of them were unfolded to
  the last definition.

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item><strong|(D1)> The hierarchy built from these data, over the Wilson
    family of the construction, has the main properties along
    <math|p<rsub|<math-up|vol>>\<circ\>q<rsub|<math-up|vol>>>. Theorem 2.1 is
    the special case described above.

    <item><strong|(D2) Contact extensions.> For every state <math|s>, every
    <math|n> and every <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>> there is a
    distribution <math|\<cal-D\>> on <math|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>,
    that is, a continuous linear functional on
    <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>|)>>
    with its usual topology, such that <math|\<cal-D\><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsup|s><around|(|a;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>
    for every <math|f> supported in <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>. Uniqueness
    is not claimed.

    <item><strong|(D3)> The construction predicate. Its parts: the bare
    couplings follow one selected RG trajectory that reaches the reference
    coupling at level <math|0>; the local lattice sections are gauge
    invariant, satisfy reality and hypercubic covariance, give <math|1> on
    the unit label, and match each classical label to Taylor order <math|d>
    on links obtained by parallel transport of polynomial potentials; the
    stencils fit in the lattice; and the transfer data of the insertions are
    normalized at a fixed reference level.

    <item><strong|(D4)> The ordered limit holds with the <em|full>
    ultraviolet sequence and the refined volume sequence, for the internal
    limit table (the same content as (P1)).

    <item><strong|(D5) Fixed-volume limits in position space.> For every
    volume index <math|p> and token list <math|v> there is a function,
    <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>>> away from periodic coincidences
    (<math|x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>\<nin\>L<rsub|p>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>> for
    <math|i\<neq\>j>), to which the smeared moments converge as
    <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> for admissible tests. Lattice difference
    quotients of the lattice correlations converge, locally uniformly away
    from periodic coincidences, to the corresponding derivatives.

    <item><strong|(D6)> The RG trajectory of the family is generated exactly
    by an explicit recursion from chosen constants and step data.

    <item><strong|(D7) Every phase.> For every phase <math|\<xi\>\<in\>X>
    there is a relativistic theory that satisfies (W0)\U(W3), with the same
    label space, and realizes the boundary values of a continuation attached
    to the phase <math|\<xi\>>. In each such theory the zero-energy subspace
    is <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, and the weak commutant of the
    fields is trivial (irreducibility). A bounded comparison map from the
    phase's Wightman space to its Osterwalder\USchrader space is surjective,
    and the Euclidean vacuum of the phase is unique. There are bounds uniform
    in the phase: low-energy spectral weight
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>><around|(|<around|{|0\<less\>p<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>>
    for <math|u\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>, and
    <math|0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|x,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-P<rsub|0>|)>*x|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>>
    for <math|s\<geqslant\>1>, for vectors <math|x=A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>
    with <math|A> a finite sum of field words. The Wightman distributions
    depend continuously on <math|\<xi\>>. The stress tensor continues to
    every phase. The <math|\<nu\>>-average of the phase Euclidean
    expectations equals the mixed expectation, and the <math|\<nu\>>-average
    of the phase Wightman distributions equals the boundary distribution of
    the analytic continuation of <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>>, on joint
    Schwartz tests. The internal form of the Wightman axioms also includes
    positivity of the Wightman functional.

    <item><strong|(D8)> Integrated forms of the OPE remainder: averages over
    complex measures on cluster\Uspectator configurations with bounded total
    variation, uniform over measurable choices of phases; and an OPE
    foundation with injectivity of the configurations involved.

    <item><strong|(D9) Trace tests.> (T1); the operator identification in
    Definition 1.14; the short-distance law (T3); existence and uniqueness of
    a spectral measure for the two-point function of <math|\<Theta\>> with a
    prescribed log-corrected high-energy scaling limit; and three
    countertests. <em|Wick:> for every finite Gaussian (Wick) model and every
    pair of polynomial fields in it, there are Schwartz functions <math|f,g>,
    subject to a compact-support condition on a transform of
    <math|f\<otimes\>g>, at which the selected Wightman two-point function of
    <math|\<Theta\>> differs from the model's two-point function.
    <em|Maxwell:> for every isometric embedding of <math|\<cal-H\>> into an
    <math|n>-colour free Maxwell Fock space that maps the vacuum to the
    vacuum, intertwines translations and has transverse symmetric image,
    there are a compactly supported <math|f> and real compactly supported
    <math|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|n>>, causally separated from <math|f>,
    with <math|<around|\<langle\>|W*<around|(|-h|)>*\<Omega\>,\<Theta\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<neq\><around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*\<Omega\>,W<around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>
    for the Weyl (coherent) vectors <math|W*<around|(|\<pm\>h|)>*\<Omega\>>
    and the images of <math|\<Theta\><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>*\<Omega\>>.
    Equality would hold if <math|\<Theta\><around|(|f|)>> commuted with the
    Weyl operators, so <math|\<Theta\>> is not a local field of that Maxwell
    theory. <em|Invariant:> an averaged version of the Maxwell countertest,
    localized in an arbitrarily small ball.

    <item><strong|(D10)> Nested OPE estimates over admissible finite trees of
    clusters, with truncation degrees and rates at the nodes, in the mixed
    state and every phase.

    <item><strong|(D11)\U(D13) Contacts.> The contact devices use the same
    OPE prescription as the reader. The full contact resolution at multiple
    collisions satisfies summation formulas and estimates and agrees with the
    separated correlation away from total collapse. There is a real basis
    system of the OPE strata containing the unit field. There is a sequence
    of contact thresholds <math|t<rsub|n>*\<downarrow\>*0> with
    <math|4*t<rsub|n+1>\<less\>t<rsub|n>> and
    <math|2*<around|(|n+1|)>*t<rsub|n>> at most the clearance at degree
    <math|n>.

    <item><strong|(D14)\U(D15) Free Maxwell comparison.> Along a scaling
    sequence <math|r<rsub|k>*\<downarrow\>*0> with field frames
    <math|U<rsub|k>>, the rescaled correlations of the mixed state, for the
    stress tensor and the other fields, converge, with all derivatives and
    uniformly on compact subsets of <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>, to those
    of <math|dim \<frak-g\>> free Maxwell fields with the Maxwell stress
    tensor [Assessment, Ÿ7.4]. The relevant space of conserved tensors is
    one-dimensional, and derivatives and rotations are compatible in the
    limit. A normalization and leading position-space comparison holds at
    degree <math|4> along the same sequence.

    <item><strong|(D16)> The family's block-spin kernel penalty equals
    <math|b>.
  </itemize-dot>

  In addition, the proof of regularity for the constructed family gives
  (Reg1) with <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=2*b<rsub|0>=11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>.
  This value is established in the proof and is not a conjunct of the
  detailed theorem. Some of the extra conclusions are the formal counterparts
  of results described in the group's assessment: Theorems 4.1, 6.1 and 7.2
  and ŸŸ7.1\U7.5 [Assessment].

  <subsection|3. Corollary: the case <math|S*U<around|(|2|)>>><label|3-corollary-the-case-su2>

  <strong|Corollary 3.1.> Let <math|G=S*U<around|(|2|)>=<around|{|g\<in\>U<around|(|2|)>:det
  g=1|}>> in its defining representation (<math|m=2>), and let
  <math|\<frak-g\>> be its Lie algebra <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*u><around|(|2|)>=<around|{|X:exp
  <around|(|s*X|)>\<in\>S*U<around|(|2|)>\<forall\>s|}>>. Then
  <math|\<frak-g\>> is simple, and the conclusion of Theorem 2.1 holds for
  <math|<around|(|2,S*U<around|(|2|)>,<with|math-font|Euler|s*u><around|(|2|)>|)>>:
  there is a regular Wilson family for <math|S*U<around|(|2|)>> such that for
  every strictly increasing <math|p> there are a strictly increasing <math|q>
  and a hierarchy with the main properties along <math|p\<circ\>q>.

  Here <math|dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>> \<frak-g\>=3>, so the constant in (T3) is
  <math|9/\<pi\><rsup|4>>. In the construction's normalization
  <math|C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>=8>, so the constructed slope in (Reg1) is
  <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=11/<around|(|3*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>. Neither number
  appears in the formal corollary; both are evaluations of the general
  formulas. Simplicity is proved from three explicit matrices spanning
  <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*u><around|(|2|)>> whose brackets show that a
  nonzero ideal contains all three.

  <subsection|4. What is not claimed><label|4-what-is-not-claimed>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Uniqueness of the infinite-volume limit, or independence of
    the subsequence.> The refinement <math|q> depends on <math|p>, and the
    hierarchy depends on <math|p> and <math|q>. Nothing relates the
    hierarchies obtained for different <math|p> or <math|q>, and nothing
    asserts convergence along the full sequence of volumes. "This
    infinite-volume limit is only taken subsequentially \<ldots\> it remains
    unresolved whether the limiting correlations depend on the chosen
    subsequence" [Assessment, Ÿ5; also p. 1 and Ÿ8, item 1]. The source
    README says: "The volume limit allows refinement."

    <item><strong|Universality in the lattice data.> The spacings
    <math|a<rsub|N>>, the sides <math|L<rsub|p>>, the couplings
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> and the insertions are produced by the
    construction. No statement concerns other choices, and none says that the
    limit is independent of them. "The construction chooses both sequences,
    and thus the UV convergence theorem does not assert convergence over all
    possible sequences of spacings"; "there is no universality result"
    [Assessment, ŸŸ4, 4.3]. The order of limits is fixed:
    <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> first at each fixed volume, then the volume along
    <math|p\<circ\>q>. No diagonal limit, no exchange of limits and no rates
    are asserted.

    <item><strong|Coincident points.> Every Euclidean statement concerns
    separated configurations; values at coincident points are unconstrained.
    The main theorem contains no distributional extension across the
    diagonals. The detailed theorem supplies one (D2), without uniqueness.
    "Uniqueness over other contact prescriptions and raw cutoff convergence
    on collision diagonals are outside the claim" [source README];
    "uniqueness, the full OS axioms for these extensions, and lattice
    convergence are not proven for these extensions" [Assessment, Ÿ7.5; also
    Ÿ10].

    <item><strong|Clustering of the mixed state; uniqueness and agreement of
    the phases.> Neither <math|X> nor <math|\<nu\>> is claimed to be trivial,
    and <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>> is not claimed to cluster. The
    relativistic theory of the main theorem is built for the selected phase
    only, and <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is not characterized by any
    property in the main theorem. Nothing says that different phases have
    equal correlations or are related by a symmetry, or that a phase is
    itself a limit of lattice states. The assessment notes that "each
    component theory correlation function <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> clusters, but
    this does not imply clustering of <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>>"
    [Assessment, Ÿ6.2], and that "agreement of the component theories [is]
    also unresolved" [Assessment, p. 1].

    <item><strong|Mass gap.> No spectral gap is asserted; (W0) is only the
    spectrum condition with uniqueness of the vacuum. "It does not prove
    \<ldots\> that there is a mass gap" [Assessment, p. 1]; "The construction
    provides no evidence for or against a mass gap" [Assessment, Ÿ2, item 4;
    also Ÿ8, item 3]; "A positive mass gap \<ldots\> [is] outside its scope"
    [source README]. The low-energy and heat bounds in (D7) are polynomial,
    not exponential.

    <item><strong|Perturbation theory to all orders.> The OPE is a
    finite-truncation asymptotic statement. Its coefficients are not compared
    with perturbation theory, and neither convergence as
    <math|D\<to\>\<infty\>> nor uniqueness of the coefficients is claimed.
    "Perturbative agreement to all orders remains unproved" [Assessment,
    Ÿ7.5; also Ÿ9.4]; "all-orders MS-bar matching [is] outside its scope"
    [source README]. Only the leading constant in (T3) has been compared with
    the one-loop prediction, and that comparison is external to the theorem
    [Assessment, Ÿ7.2].

    <item><strong|Euclidean axioms.> The main theorem asserts no Euclidean
    covariance, reflection positivity, growth bound or cluster property for
    the correlation functions <math|S<rsup|s>>. These are proved inside the
    construction and checked independently for the selected trace hierarchy
    [Assessment, Ÿ10], but they are not conclusions of Theorem 2.1.

    <item><strong|Field content and lattice meaning.> The main theorem does
    not say that the tokens and insertions are lattice versions of
    gauge-invariant polynomials in the curvature, that <math|F<rsub|d>> is a
    quotient of a space of such polynomials, or how large <math|F<rsub|d>>
    is. It contains no stress tensor and does not identify <math|\<Theta\>>
    as the trace of one. These are properties of the construction, recorded
    in part in the detailed theorem (Remark 2.4, (D3), (D9), (D14)). The
    group reports that OpenAI informed them that the public target "omitted
    some of the connections to the lattice construction established by its
    proof" [Assessment, Ÿ9].

    <item><strong|The running coupling.> Only the existence of a positive
    slope <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>> in (Reg1) is claimed. The value
    <math|11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>, and
    asymptotic freedom of a renormalized effective coupling [Assessment,
    Ÿ7.4, Theorem 7.2], are not part of Theorem 2.1. The effective coupling
    is not proved independent of the subsequential limit [Assessment, Ÿ7.4].

    <item><strong|Free fields.> Only finite Wick profiles (Definition 1.15)
    are excluded, and only for <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>>. Generalized free
    fields with continuous mass spectrum, infinite sums, and general
    composites of Gaussian fields are not excluded. The theorem "does not
    rule out every description in terms of composites of Gaussian fields"
    [Assessment, Ÿ7.3]; "general exclusion of continuous-mass generalized
    free fields \<ldots\> [is] outside its scope" [source README].

    <item><strong|Other objects.> There is no probability measure on a space
    of distributions [Assessment, Ÿ3 and Ÿ8, item 2], no Wilson loop
    observable or area law [Ÿ8, item 4], and no particle spectrum or
    scattering theory [Ÿ8, item 5].

    <item><strong|Analyticity.> Only analyticity in the complex times at
    fixed real spatial positions is asserted (R13). Analyticity in the full
    forward tube, with complex spatial coordinates, is not asserted. The
    Wightman functions are identified as boundary values only on product test
    functions (R15).

    <item><strong|Uniqueness of the hierarchy for a given volume sequence.>
    Apart from Remark 2.3, no part of the hierarchy is claimed to be unique.
  </enumerate>

  <subsection|5. Comparison with the standard
  formulations><label|5-comparison-with-the-standard-formulations>

  <subsubsection|5.1 Wightman axioms [SW]><label|51-wightman-axioms-sw>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Standard
  requirement>>|<cell|<strong|Form in Theorem
  2.1>>|<cell|<strong|Departure>>>|<row|<cell|Strongly continuous unitary
  representation of the covering group of
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>>|<cell|<math|U> on
  <math|\<cal-H\>>, a representation of <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>
  (Definition 1.9, (W2))>|<cell|No double cover; no spinor
  fields>>|<row|<cell|Spectrum condition>|<cell|(W0a)\U(W0b): a
  projection-valued measure related to <math|T> by Fourier transform in the
  Euclidean pairing, supported in <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>>|<cell|Equivalent
  after <math|<wide|p|\<vect\>>\<mapsto\>-<wide|p|\<vect\>>>>>|<row|<cell|Invariant
  vacuum, unique up to a factor>|<cell|(W2c), (W0c),
  (W1a)>|<cell|None>>|<row|<cell|Fields are operator-valued tempered
  distributions on a dense invariant domain containing
  <math|\<Omega\>>>|<cell|(W1b), (W1d); invariance of <math|D> is built into
  the definition>|<cell|Norm continuity in <math|f>, stronger than weak
  temperedness>>|<row|<cell|Cyclicity of the vacuum>|<cell|(W1b) with
  (W1c)>|<cell|<math|D> <em|equals> the span of the field monomials on
  <math|\<Omega\>>>>|<row|<cell|Finitely many field components transforming
  under a finite-dimensional representation of the Lorentz
  group>|<cell|Abstract label space <math|\<cal-L\>> with a linear
  representation <math|\<rho\>> of <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>>|<cell|<math|\<cal-L\>>
  may be infinite-dimensional; <math|\<rho\>> need not be finite-dimensional,
  continuous, or trivial on translations>>|<row|<cell|Hermiticity>|<cell|(W1e),
  with an antilinear map <math|\<ell\>\<mapsto\>\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>>>|<cell|<math|\<ell\>\<mapsto\>\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  need not be an involution>>|<row|<cell|Local commutativity>|<cell|(W3),
  commutators for all pairs of labels>|<cell|Bosonic fields
  only>>|<row|<cell|(not standard)>|<cell|(R2) faithful fields; (R3)\U(R6)
  relations between fields, labels and lattice tokens>|<cell|Additional
  requirements>>>>>

  The relativistic theory is constructed for <strong|one phase>
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>, and its Wightman functions are tied to the
  Euclidean data only through (R13)\U(R15). These clauses concern analyticity
  in the times alone and boundary values on product test functions.

  <subsubsection|5.2 Osterwalder\USchrader axioms
  [OS]><label|52-osterwalderschrader-axioms-os>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Axiom>>|<cell|<strong|Status
  in Theorem 2.1>>>|<row|<cell|E0: Schwinger functions are (tempered)
  distributions>|<cell|Not asserted. The <math|S<rsup|s>> are functions on
  <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>, <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>>> there (R10), with
  no growth bound, tested only against <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>
  in (P1). The extension (D2) is in the detailed theorem
  only.>>|<row|<cell|E0\<prime\>: linear growth in the number of
  points>|<cell|Not asserted>>|<row|<cell|E1: Euclidean covariance>|<cell|Not
  asserted>>|<row|<cell|E2: reflection positivity>|<cell|Not
  asserted>>|<row|<cell|E3: permutation symmetry>|<cell|(R9), on
  <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>>>|<row|<cell|E4: cluster property>|<cell|Not
  asserted for any state. In its place: the phase decomposition (R7), (R8),
  (R11), (R12), and uniqueness of the vacuum (W0c) in the selected phase's
  relativistic theory.>>|<row|<cell|Reconstruction theorem>|<cell|Not stated
  as a theorem. Its conclusion is asserted directly for the selected phase:
  (R13)\U(R15) together with (W0)\U(W3).>>>>>

  The group checked separately, outside Theorem 2.1, that the selected-phase
  correlations of <math|\<Theta\>> satisfy E0\UE4 and E0\<prime\> in the
  formulation of [DMY], on Schwartz tests that vanish to all orders on the
  diagonals [Assessment, Ÿ10].

  <subsubsection|5.3 The Jaffe\UWitten formulation
  [JW]><label|53-the-jaffewitten-formulation-jw>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Requirement
  in [JW]>>|<cell|<strong|Status in Theorem 2.1>>>|<row|<cell|A compact
  simple gauge group <math|G>>|<cell|Definition 1.1: a closed subgroup of
  <math|U<around|(|m|)>> with simple Lie algebra. This covers every compact
  simple Lie group in a faithful unitary representation, and also some
  disconnected groups.>>|<row|<cell|A quantum field theory on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>> satisfying axioms at least as strong as Wightman's
  or Osterwalder\USchrader's>|<cell|Wightman axioms in the form (W0)\U(W3),
  for the relativistic theory of one selected phase of a subsequential
  infinite-volume limit. [JW] regards a subsequential limit obtained by
  compactness as insufficient unless further arguments establish the required
  properties [Assessment, Ÿ5].>>|<row|<cell|Nontriviality>|<cell|(T3)\U(T4)>>|<row|<cell|Local
  fields corresponding to gauge-invariant local polynomials in the curvature
  and its covariant derivatives>|<cell|Abstract spaces <math|F<rsub|d>> with
  lattice origin ((P1), (R6)). The identification with curvature polynomials
  belongs to the construction (Ÿ4, item 8).>>|<row|<cell|Short-distance
  behaviour predicted by asymptotic freedom: stress tensor, OPE with the
  predicted singularities>|<cell|(Reg1): logarithmic growth of the bare
  inverse coupling, slope unspecified. (O1)\U(O2): finite-order OPE bounds,
  not matched to perturbation theory. (T3): leading law of the trace
  two-point function with the one-loop constant. The stress tensor appears
  only in the construction.>>|<row|<cell|Mass gap
  <math|\<Delta\>\<gtr\>0>>|<cell|Not claimed>>>>>

  In summary, the formulation differs from the standard ones in four
  structural ways. (i) Euclidean statements concern separated configurations
  only. (ii) The relativistic fields are faithful, labelled by an abstract
  space built from the quotient spaces <math|F<rsub|d>>. (iii) A phase
  decomposition replaces a clustering requirement on the mixed Euclidean
  limit; the selected phase's relativistic theory has a unique invariant
  vacuum. (iv) Limits are taken along construction-chosen lattice sequences
  and a subsequence of volumes.

